{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>The scepter shall not depart from Judah</b> — its meaning is not that it shall never depart, for it is written: \"YHWH will bring you, and your king whom you shall set over you, to a nation that neither you nor your fathers have known\" (Deuteronomy 28:36), and behold they and their king are in exile, they have no longer a king or princes, and for many days there was no king in Israel. And the prophet would not promise Israel that they shall never go into captivity for any reason because Judah shall rule over them. Rather, its meaning is that the scepter shall not depart from Judah to one of his brothers, for the kingship of Israel that rules over them shall come from him, and no brother shall rule over him. And likewise the lawgiver shall not depart from between his feet — every lawgiver in Israel who holds the king's signet shall come from him, for he shall rule and command all Israel, and his is the seal of the kingship, until Shiloh comes, and to him shall the gathering of the peoples be, to do with them all according to his will — and this is the Messiah. For \"the scepter\" alludes to David, who is the first king to whom belongs the scepter of kingship, and \"Shiloh\" is his son, to whom shall be the gathering of the peoples. And the statement of Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra], that Shiloh should be David, is not possible, for Judah had no scepter before David, for although his tribe was honored and traveled first, there is no scepter except for a king and ruler, as it is written: \"The scepter of equity is the scepter of your kingdom\" (Psalms 45:7), \"the scepter of the rulers\" (Isaiah 14:5), \"a scepter to rule\" (Ezekiel 19:14). And this verse hints that Jacob invested the tribe of Judah with sovereignty over his brothers, and bequeathed to Judah the dominion over Israel — this is what David said: \"And YHWH, the Elohim of Israel, chose me from all my father's house to be king over Israel forever, for He chose Judah as leader, and from the house of Judah my father's house, and among my father's sons He was pleased to make me king over all Israel\" (I Chronicles 28:4). And He said \"shall not depart\" to hint that another tribe would rule over Israel, but from the time Judah begins to have the scepter of kingship, it shall not depart from him to another tribe, and this is what is said: \"For YHWH the Elohim of Israel gave the kingdom to David over Israel forever, to him and to his sons\" (II Chronicles 13:5). And the matter of Saul was thus: because the matter of requesting a king at that time was detestable before the Holy One, blessed be He, He did not wish to appoint over them the tribe to which the kingship belonged, which would never depart from it forever, and He gave them a fleeting kingship. And therefore Scripture hinted, saying: \"I will give you a king in My wrath, and I will take him away in My wrath\" (Hosea 13:11) — He gave him [the kingship] unwillingly, and therefore He took him away in His wrath, for he and his sons were killed and the kingship was cut off from him. And all this was because Samuel was judge and prophet, waging their wars by the word of YHWH and delivering them, and they had no business asking for a king in his days, as he said to them: \"But YHWH your Elohim was your king\" (I Samuel 12:12). And the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: \"They have not rejected you, but Me they have rejected from reigning over them\" (I Samuel 8:7), and therefore He did not give them an everlasting kingship. And what Scripture says: \"You have acted foolishly; you have not kept the commandment of YHWH your Elohim that He commanded you, for now YHWH would have established your kingdom over Israel forever\" (I Samuel 13:13) — had he not sinned, there would have been kingship for his seed in Israel, not over all of them, and this is the meaning of \"over Israel\"; or perhaps he would have ruled over his mother's tribes, over Benjamin and Ephraim and Manasseh, for Judah and Ephraim were reckoned like two nations in Israel; or he would have been a king under the hand of the king of Judah. And in my opinion, the kings who ruled over Israel from the remaining tribes after David were transgressing the will of their father [Jacob] and transferring the inheritance [to those not entitled], and they were relying on the words of Ahijah the Shilonite the prophet, who anointed Jeroboam and said: \"And I will afflict the seed of David for this, but not for all days\" (I Kings 11:39). And as Israel persisted in appointing over themselves kings from the remaining tribes, one king after another, and did not return to the kingship of Judah, they transgressed the command of the patriarch and were punished for it, as Hosea said: \"They have set up kings, but not from Me\" (Hosea 8:4). And this was the punishment of the Hasmoneans, who reigned in the Second Temple, for they were devout men of the Most High, and had it not been for them the Torah and commandments would have been forgotten from Israel — and even so they were punished with a great punishment, for four sons of Mattathias the devout elder reigned one after another, and despite all their might and success, they fell by the sword of their enemies. And in the end the punishment reached what the Sages, blessed be their memory, said (Bava Batra 3a): \"Whoever says, 'I am of the house of the Hasmonean' — he is a slave,\" for they were all cut off on account of this sin. And although there was punishment of the seed of Simeon among the righteous, nevertheless all the seed of Mattathias the Hasmonean, the righteous man, perished only because they reigned though they were not of the seed of Judah and of the house of David, and they removed the scepter and the lawgiver entirely. And their punishment was measure for measure: the Holy One, blessed be He, set their slaves over them, and those destroyed them. And it is also possible that there was upon them a sin in their kingship because they were priests, and they were commanded: \"You shall keep your priesthood for every matter of the altar and within the veil, and you shall serve; the priesthood I give as a gift\" (Numbers 18:7), and it was not for them to reign, but only to perform the service of YHWH. And I found in the Jerusalem Talmud in tractate Horayot (Jerusalem Talmud, Horayot 3:2): \"They do not anoint priests as kings.\" Rabbi Yehuda said: this is learned from the verse \"The scepter shall not depart from Judah.\" Rabbi Ḥiyya bar Abba said: from \"that his days may be long in his kingdom, he and his sons, in the midst of Israel\" — what is written after it? \"He shall not be for the priests the Levites.\" Behold, they taught here that they do not anoint kings from the priests, the sons of Aaron. And he explained at the outset that this is for the honor of Judah, that the dominion does not depart from that tribe. Therefore, even though Israel may appoint over themselves a king from the remaining tribes according to the need of the hour, they do not anoint them, so that the splendor of kingship should not rest upon them; rather they serve merely as judges and officers. And they mentioned the priests because although they themselves are worthy of anointment, they are not anointed for kingship, and all the more so the remaining tribes, as they said in the Gemara (Horayot 11a), that they anoint only kings of the house of David. And Rabbi Ḥiyya bar Abba explained that it is withheld by the Torah that the priests the Levites — all the tribe of Levi — shall have no portion or inheritance in the kingship, and this is a fitting and proper thing." "<b>And to him shall be the gathering of peoples</b> — the gathering of the peoples, as it is said: \"To him shall the nations inquire\" (Isaiah 11:10). And similar to it: \"An eye that mocks a father and scorns the gathering (יקהת) of a mother\" (Proverbs 30:17) — the gathering of wrinkles in her face because of old age. And in the Talmud (Yevamot 110a): \"They raise a קהיאתא in the marketplace of Nehardea.\" And one could say קהיית עמים, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 49:10). And it does not seem correct to interpret \"יקהת אם\" as the gathering of a mother. And the expression \"מקהו קהיאתא\" means nothing other than objections and difficulties, for they would raise and refute many difficulties against it; one who presses a difficulty is called קוה in the language of the Sages, as they said in Midrash Ḥazita (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 3:8), \"all of them graspers of the sword\" — that they all studied halakhah as sharp as a sword, so that if a case came to their hands, the halakhah would not be קוה for them. And so too many others there. And from this they said in the Gemara (Nazir 65b): \"Rabbi Yehoshua pressed (קהה) and declared pure\" — he raised many difficulties against it and broke all the words of those who declared it impure until he was forced to declare it pure. And likewise we find in old versions of the Gemara (Bava Metzia 52a): \"One who presses (דקהי) against the coins is called an evil person\" — one who is exacting and presses, reluctant to accept it from his fellow. And the grammarians (the Radak and Rabbi Yonah ibn Janach) say regarding יקהת that its root is יקה, and they interpreted it as an expression of obedience and acceptance of the commandment: יקהת עמים — that they will obey him and do all that he commands them; תבוז ליקהת אם — to accept her commandment. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it is from the expression \"the one who eats unripe fruit, his teeth will be set on edge (תקהנה)\" (Jeremiah 31:28), and its root is קהה, and the י in it is like the י in יצהר, and the meaning of all of them is weakening and breaking: he says that a taskmaster's scepter shall not depart from Judah until his son comes, to whom belongs the weakening and breaking of the peoples, who will weaken all of them by the sword. And likewise: \"If the iron is blunt (קהה)\" (Ecclesiastes 10:10) — it has grown weak and cannot cut, like a knife that has lost its edge in the language of the Sages (Beitzah 28a), or one that has broken somewhat and developed nicks. And so I found there again in Midrash Ḥazita (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 1:12): the Holy One, blessed be He, caused a fragrant aroma to waft before them from the spices of the Garden of Eden, and their souls grew faint (קוהה) to eat; they said to him: Our master Moses, give us something to eat! He said to them: Thus said the Holy One, blessed be He: No foreigner shall eat of it. They arose and separated the foreigners from among them, and their souls grew faint (קוהה) to eat, etc. And the meaning is that their souls grew weak and faint in their bodies from the intensity of their desire to eat of the Paschal lamb, in which the good aroma was embedded. And so too: \"Let not the halakhah be קוה for them\" — weak and flimsy in their hands. And מקהו קהיאתא — those who press difficulties, from the weakness of the soul due to much distress and contemplation; or it is an expression of breaking and refuting, as in the language of (Kiddushin 13b) פריך רב אחא. And so they said (Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael, Bo 18): \"You too — blunt (הקהה) his teeth\" — break them or weaken them with your words. For unripe fruit weakens but does not break; rather, weakening and breaking are one concept, and קהיה includes them both."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The phrase רז״ל (Rabbotenu Zikhronam Livrakhah, 'our Sages of blessed memory') is rendered as 'the Sages, blessed be their memory'; רבי אברהם refers to Ibn Ezra. The heading of the Jerusalem Talmud passage is attributed to Rabbi Yehuda and his colleague by name in the text; the Aramaic formula ענתוריא (a technical term for a scriptural support by analogy) is rendered 'this is learned from the verse.'", 1 "The lemma and verse citations follow the supplied Hebrew text; Proverbs 30:17 and the phrase תבוז ליקהת אם are reproduced exactly as Ramban quotes them. The Mekhilta passage (בא יח) is normalized parenthetically to its standard parashah-numbering formula, as Ramban's Hebrew uses the chapter-like heading convention for Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:23:08.338891184Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}